use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Dismissal import Dismissal
from ../../../behaviors/Modality import Modality
from ../../../behaviors/Presence import Presence

export ui-component Dialog:
    description:
        A dialog is a window that opens on top of the page for a task the user
        deals with before going back, such as confirming a choice or filling in
        a short form. While it's open, the rest of the page can't be used. It
        is not attached to anything; it usually sits in the middle of the
        screen.

    behaviors:
        - @{Modality}
        - @{Dismissal}
        - @{Presence}

    opening:
        The component using the dialog decides what opens it, usually a
        button, and can also open it itself. Several buttons can open the same
        dialog; the one that was used is the one focus goes back to.

    blocking:
        While it's open, the dialog blocks the rest of the page the way
        Modality describes. Focus stays inside it, everything behind it can't
        be used and is hidden from assistive tools, and the page behind
        doesn't scroll. A dimmed backdrop usually shows this. The component
        can make a dialog non-modal instead, so the page behind stays usable,
        but such a dialog still isn't attached to anything; one that is
        attached is a Popover.

    titleAndDescription:
        A dialog has a name, normally its visible title, which assistive tools
        read out when it opens, along with a short description if it has one.
        A dialog with no visible title still has a name.

    focus:
        When it opens, focus moves to the first thing inside that can take
        focus, unless the component names something else, such as the least
        risky button. For a long piece of text, focus can go to the start of
        the text instead so it's read from the top. Tab and Shift+Tab move
        through the dialog and wrap from the last item to the first.

    closing:
        It closes the ways Dismissal describes: Escape, or clicking the
        backdrop. It usually has a close button too. The component can turn
        any of these off, for example while unsaved changes would be lost. Focus then goes back
        to whatever opened it, or somewhere else the component names.

    alert:
        A dialog can be marked as an alert, for urgent messages that need an
        answer, such as "Delete 12 files?". Assistive tools announce it as an
        alert. It has at least two choices, focus starts on the least
        destructive one, and clicking the backdrop doesn't close it.

    stacking:
        A dialog can open another on top of it. Only the top one can be used.
        Escape closes only the top one, and focus goes back to where it was in
        the one underneath.

    nonGoals:
        - Not for messages that need no answer. A short note that goes away by
            itself is a Toast
        - Doesn't slide in from an edge or get dragged. That's a Drawer
        - Its size and position are fixed. A window the user can move and
            resize is a FloatingPanel
